## Conversation
User wanted Kimi-K3 servable on GB300 in inference-dev-eu-n-04a, private-only
(no public pushes), smallest change set. Built the image via ad-hoc buildx
--build-arg overrides first; user then noticed the version/dep bumps existed
only as args, not in code, and asked to rebase onto ace/vllm-0.26.0 (PR #193)
and encode them properly. This commit is that encoding.

## Actions
Fetched PR #193 as refs/pull/193/head -> k3/ace-base (75cc069), branched
abatilo/vllm-kimi-k3 from it, stashed local Dockerfile edits, resolved one
conflict, added the matrix entry + workflow build-arg.

## Errors & Mistakes
- Stash pop conflicted: I had made quack-kernels an ARG defaulting to 0.4.1;
  ace set it to 0.6.1 outright and rewrote the same comment block. Dropped
  mine entirely -- theirs is simpler and CI-validated.
- First version of the patch guard was `[ "$X" = "1" ]`. That silently breaks
  ace's entries: the workflow's build-args block is static, so entries
  omitting apply-flashinfer-patch render `APPLY_FLASHINFER_PATCH=` (empty),
  which is != "1" and would have skipped the patch for 0.6.14 builds. Changed
  to skip only on explicit 0 via `${X:-1}` and verified the truth table for
  '1'/''/'0'/unset.
- Claimed `docker/AGENTS.md`'s "no native arm64 builder" blocked arm64 builds.
  That doc covers the `at` repo's bake builders (picanha/zain), NOT
  ml-containers CI, which builds arm64 fine -- PR #193 pushed merged
  multi-arch manifests. Two different build systems; I conflated them.

## Dead Ends
- FlashInfer private-cubin path for SiTU MoE: public 0.6.15.post1 has no
  is_private, no FLASHINFER_PRIVATE_CUBIN_DIR and no ActivationType.Situ
  (checked through v0.6.16rc1 and upstream main). vLLM's
  trtllm_mxfp4_moe.py:146/264/382 needs all three. Not fixable publicly.
- tokenspeed-situ on PyPI ("AOT TensorRT-LLM-Gen MXFP4+SiTU MoE kernel
  bundle") is x86_64-only -- no aarch64 wheel, useless on GB300.
- COPY --from the upstream image to steal its FlashInfer: their Situ = 10
  collides with public InvalidType = 10, and SGLang documents that a mixed
  Python/cubin/JIT-cache install fails at import. Abandoned.

## Hints for Future Agents
- setuptools_scm reports this branch as 0.23.1rc1.dev1483+g658f2f56e. That is
  NOT old code. vLLM tags releases off release branches, so no modern tag is
  an ancestor; `git describe` walks back to v0.23.1rc0, 1483 commits. By
  content the branch is 204 ahead of / 12 behind v0.26.0. Upstream's own K3
  image reports 0.1.dev19262 (no tag reachable at all), so neither satisfies
  the recipe's declared min_vllm_version: 0.26.0.
- sm_103a lands via NVCC_APPEND_FLAGS + the arch_list sed, NOT via the
  per-target CMake arch list. `FlashKDA CUDA architectures: 9.0a;10.0a;12.0a`
  in the log is the CUDA_ARCHS intersection and is misleading -- cuobjdump
  --list-elf on the built .so is the only trustworthy check. Note
  NVCC_WRAPPER_FILTER_CODES strips the non-`a` variants.
- Upstream's vllm/vllm-openai:kimi-k3 has NO sm_103 cubin in _flashkda_C
  (only sm_90a, sm_100, sm_120) and was built from commit b6bbf29dd, which
  does not exist publicly. Behavioural differences vs ours are not
  necessarily our bug.
- buildx clips its own progress log at 2MiB. Use --progress rawjson or read
  `buildctl debug histories` in the buildkitd pod instead of wc -l on the log.
- A --cache-from pointing at a not-yet-existing cache tag is a HARD failure,
  not a warning. Omit it on the first build.

## Codebase Discoveries
- sccache-start.sh:27 is `: "${AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID:?} ${AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY:?}"`
  -- a hard requirement. Local builds need
  `--secret id=s3_access_key_id,env=AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` (+ secret key). The
  `default` AWS profile can read s3://ml-dev-ci-cache (604,912 objects under
  the vllm-tensorizer prefix).
- Direct buildx invocation needs `--build-context common=../common`; the
  Dockerfile does `COPY --from=common`, supplied by the workflow's
  build-contexts.
- cache-key is derived from cuda+ubuntu only, so the new torch-2.13 entry
  SHARES cuda13.2.1-ubuntu24.04 with the torch-2.11 entry. Wasteful, not
  incorrect (BuildKit still matches layer inputs). Adding torch to the
  cache-key filter would fix it but invalidates ace's warm cache, so left
  alone deliberately.

